From 3a7d46b46d92325f8913aaaafac206c0f802ead0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 23:05:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Make the fallback font's glyph caches thread safe
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

FallbackSystemFont is a process-wide singleton whose glyph-index and advance
caches were plain Dictionary instances written with no synchronization. Text
measurement is not confined to one thread, so two threads measuring at once
tore the buckets and threw IndexOutOfRangeException from inside
Dictionary.TryInsert — a stack trace that points at the font code rather than
at whatever was measuring.

It surfaced as Broiler.UI's toolbar tests failing intermittently, since xunit
runs an assembly's test classes in parallel and several of them measure button
text. Measured before attributing it: the same suite flakes on a clean tree
(1 of 6 runs) as well as with the caller's changes (2 of 6), so it predates
them. With ConcurrentDictionary the suite passed 8 of 8.

The regression test is checked both ways rather than just written: it fails 5
of 5 runs against the plain-Dictionary version and passes 5 of 5 with the fix.
That took a second attempt — the obvious Parallel.For version passed even with
the bug present, because the singleton gives a process exactly one cold window
in which inserts race, and by the time the loop spread out the cache was warm.
The committed version lines threads up on a Barrier and has them insert
distinct interleaved keys, so they collide inside that one window.
